\title{Completeness}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-19}

\p{
    An honest prover with a true statement is accepted, except with small probability — usually with
    probability one. Unlike [soundness](dhsorens-002F) this is one property, not a family, and
    [compilation](dhsorens-004S) preserves it. The obligation that fails is one layer down: a
    circuit is complete when every honest execution has a satisfying witness, which is what
    [Clean](dhsorens-002Y) treats as separate from soundness.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{The definition, and the completeness error. When a protocol is perfectly complete and
        when it is not.}
        \li{How completeness composes under [oracle reductions](dhsorens-002I).}
        \li{What [Fiat–Shamir](dhsorens-002G) and [BCS](dhsorens-002H) do to it.}
        \li{Circuit completeness versus protocol completeness, and the form of the obligation in
        [Clean](dhsorens-002Y).}
        \li{Where [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040) states it.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: Thaler, \em{Proofs, Arguments, and Zero-Knowledge};
    [ArkLib](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib) (\code{OracleReduction}).
}
